  "name": null,
  "problem": "Given that $k\\ge 1$ is an integer, simplify $\\frac{(k+2)!}{(k-1)!}.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $\\frac{(k+2)!}{(k-1)!}=\\,$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}",
  "worked_solution": "First, notice the following:\n\n$$\n(k+2)!=(k+2)\\cdot (k+1)\\cdot k\\cdot (k-1)!\n$$\n\nThen, we cancel the $(k-1)!$ that occurs in both the numerator and the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(k+2)!}{(k-1)!} & =\\frac{(k+2)\\cdot (k+1)\\cdot k\\cdot (k-1)!}{(k-1)!} \\\\  & =\\frac{(k+2)\\cdot (k+1)\\cdot k\\cdot \\cancel{(k-1)!}}{\\cancel{(k-1)!}} \\\\  & =(k+2)\\cdot (k+1)\\cdot k \\\\  & =k({k}^{2}+3k+2) \\\\  & ={k}^{3}+3{k}^{2}+2k\\end{aligned}\n$$",
